# Git Hands-On Lab

Objective
The objective of this hands-on is to learn the basic Git commands such as git init, git status, git add, git commit, git push and git pull. It also helps in understanding how to create a local repository and connect it with a remote GitHub repository.

Software Used:
Git Bash
GitHub
Notepad++

Procedure:

1. First I checked whether Git was installed on my system.
Command:
git --version

2. Then I configured my Git username and email.
Commands:
git config --global user.name "Hema Sree"
git config --global user.email "hemasreedangeti@gmail.com"
To verify the configuration:
git config --global --list

3. Installed Notepad++ and configured it as the default Git editor.
Commands:
"/c/Program Files/Notepad++/notepad++.exe"
git config --global core.editor "'C:/Program Files/Notepad++/notepad++.exe' -multiInst -notabbar -nosession -noPlugin"
git config --global core.editor

4. Created a new project folder named GitDemo.
Commands:
cd OneDrive
cd Desktop
mkdir GitDemo
cd GitDemo
git init
ls -la

5. Created a text file named welcome.txt.
Command:
echo "Welcome to Git Hands-On Lab" > welcome.txt
To verify the file:
ls
To display the contents:
cat welcome.txt
Checked the repository status:
git status

6. Added the file to the staging area.
Command:
git add welcome.txt
Checked the status again.
Command:
git status

7. Committed the file to the local repository.
Command:
git commit
Commit message used:
Added welcome.txt file

8. Connected the local repository with the GitHub repository.
Commands:
git remote add origin https://github.com/hemasreedangeti/GitDemo.git
git remote -v
git branch -M main

9. Pushed the local repository to GitHub.
Command:
git push -u origin main

Result:
Successfully configured Git, created a local repository, added a file, committed the changes, connected the local repository to GitHub and pushed the project successfully.
